WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.340
When a receptor grips a ligand anchored in another cell's membrane, how can both

2
00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:08.360
cells get a message?

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.260
Most signals we learn float freely across space.

4
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.100
Once a free ligand leaves its home cell, that connection is gone.

5
00:00:18.183 --> 00:00:21.083
Now tether that ligand in its own cell's membrane.

6
00:00:22.403 --> 00:00:26.123
When a receptor binds it, the receptor's cell gets the forward signal.

7
00:00:27.343 --> 00:00:29.903
The ligand's cell can get a reverse signal too.

8
00:00:31.223 --> 00:00:32.523
Why still call it a ligand?

9
00:00:33.683 --> 00:00:36.883
Those names only record which role biologists saw first.

10
00:00:37.933 --> 00:00:40.393
Sending a message inward takes two things.

11
00:00:41.753 --> 00:00:44.593
First, the ligand must stay attached to its own membrane.

12
00:00:45.873 --> 00:00:48.193
Second, it needs a path into the cytosol.

13
00:00:49.373 --> 00:00:53.193
A cytosolic tail is what you normally expect on a receptor.

14
00:00:54.553 --> 00:00:58.133
A membrane ligand can signal inward through a tail or partner.

15
00:00:59.233 --> 00:01:02.053
Start with the tail route in B-type ephrins.

16
00:01:03.273 --> 00:01:07.453
Each crosses the membrane once and ends in a short cytosolic tail.

17
00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:10.593
That tail has no kinase enzyme.

18
00:01:12.053 --> 00:01:14.373
Neither does the receptor's outer binding tip.

19
00:01:15.653 --> 00:01:19.733
Yet receptor contact puts phosphate onto tyrosines in that tail.

20
00:01:20.733 --> 00:01:24.133
How does phosphate appear with no kinase on either protein?

21
00:01:25.453 --> 00:01:28.553
Receptor binding pulls ephrins into dense clusters.

22
00:01:30.013 --> 00:01:34.613
Clustered tails draw Src-family kinases from inside the ephrin's own cell.

23
00:01:35.853 --> 00:01:37.773
Those kinases add the phosphates.

24
00:01:38.953 --> 00:01:42.193
An adaptor then docks there and links the tail to actin.

25
00:01:43.183 --> 00:01:48.483
The same tail carries a second handle at its very tip, a PDZ-binding site.

26
00:01:49.723 --> 00:01:54.163
After a delay, a phosphatase binds that end and strips the phosphates off.

27
00:01:55.483 --> 00:01:59.763
Other PDZ proteins also bind there, giving the tail a second route.

28
00:02:00.817 --> 00:02:04.297
Now watch what happens when the tail disappears completely.

29
00:02:05.497 --> 00:02:10.157
An A-type ephrin has no transmembrane segment and no cytosolic tail.

30
00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:15.977
A lipid called a GPI anchor holds it only in the outer membrane layer.

31
00:02:17.277 --> 00:02:19.957
When a receptor clusters it, how could a signal cross?

32
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.380
It borrows a partner that reaches the inside, because a signal can only enter

33
00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.180
through something touching the cytosol.

34
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:40.140
In some cells, a membrane-spanning protein such as p75 pairs with the ephrin.

35
00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.200
In cholesterol-rich patches, inner-leaflet kinases like Fyn gather underneath.

36
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:51.300
How a lipid patch alone crosses the bilayer remains unresolved.

37
00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:57.660
Different ligands solve the membrane barrier in different ways, yet the logic stays

38
00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:03.920
the same. Stay anchored at the surface, and couple receptor grip to an inward path.

39
00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:09.800
Whether through a tail or a partner, a membrane ligand can listen as well as speak.
